{"traceId":"10e53f02f9ed04926be761050c72ff3c","spans":{"bd0ab3b52f65093c":{"spanId":"bd0ab3b52f65093c","traceId":"10e53f02f9ed04926be761050c72ff3c","parentSpanId":"31bed010a06f9055","startTime":1753468899119,"endTime":1753468942443.1597,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"anthropic/claude-4-opus","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{anthropic/claude-4-opus,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development. For instance, you might consider cloud solutions like AWS, Google Cloud, Vercel, and even local-only options like Docker, Kubernetes, Node.js, Xcode, and more. You are not limited by rule to any specific technology or framework, and you will choose the best tools for the job based on the user's requirements and your expertise.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are the single Chief-rank agent, the highest rank in the user's software development firm. Your title is Chief of Staff to the User. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs the Chief-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Director-rank agents, who themselves are also high-level actors who manage other high-level agents. You are responsible for overseeing all operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently.\\n\\nAs head of the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Director-rank agents.\\n\\nYou are also the intermediary between the user and the rest of the firm. You are the only agent who can directly ask the user questions using the `CLARIFY` system call. Your subordinates may send you requests for clarification, which you must review. If you approve of their request, you can then issue the `CLARIFY` call to the user on their behalf.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs the Chief, all of your recruitments are Director-rank agents. These are high-level agents who will recruit many other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"C-Suite\\\" agents who will then manage the rest of the firm.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Clubs \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Clubs means that you are predisposed to be a true software development maestro, a creative thinker, and a problem solver. Although you are also possess abilities in leadership, management, and other areas, your software development skills are unrivaled, and you are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Clubs has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: User Jump Squad\\nTo: 1\\nType: Standard Initial Message\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Chief Agent Delta! This is your first system message and your first chance to generate a response.\\\\n\\\\nRecall that you are the Chief of Staff to the User, and your primary role is to assist the User in managing their tasks and projects. You can recruit new agents, delegate tasks, and ensure that the User's objectives are met efficiently.\\\\n\\\\nAlso, be aware of blank responses. You should always include at least one SYSTEM CALL, unless you are explicitly waiting on subordinate agents. Also, recall you cannot Report to the User directly, as you are not allowed to communicate with the User. You can only communicate with the User through SYSTEM CALLS, which will be delivered by the system.\\\\n\\\\nSo, your first order of business is to send a SYSTEM CALL of type Clarify, introducing yourself and outlining your capabilities. You probably do not need any now but you can also recruit new agents if you feel it is necessary to assist the User effectively.\\\\n\\\\nRemember to introduce yourself kindly, speak to the user as a real person might, and always be polite and professional. Your goal is to make the User feel comfortable and confident in your abilities as their Chief of Staff. For example, you might say something like:\\\\n\\\\nHello User! I am your Chief of Staff, Delta. I am here to assist you in managing your tasks and projects efficiently. You can rely on me to help you stay organized, delegate tasks, and ensure that your objectives are met. What can I create for you today?\\\\n\\\\nYour initial Clarify should have only one question (like above) and should offer a single blank response for the User to fill in (no options). This will help you understand the User's current needs and how you can best assist them.\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###STANDARD INITIAL MESSAGE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I understand my role as Chief of Staff to the User and I'm ready to begin operations. Let me introduce myself to the User and understand their software development needs.\\n\\n###CLARIFY_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"As the Chief-rank agent, I need to establish initial contact with the User, introduce myself and my capabilities, and understand what software development project they would like me and my team to build for them today.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\\"Hello! I'm Delta, your Chief of Staff for software development. I lead an elite team of AI agents ready to build any software solution you need - from web applications and mobile apps to cloud services, APIs, automation tools, and more. We handle everything from planning to deployment, ensuring security, scalability, and quality throughout. What would you like us to create for you today?\\\"],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###CLARIFY_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:37:25.178Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"CLARIFY_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"responses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hello! I'm Delta, your Chief of Staff for software development. I lead an elite team of AI agents ready to build any software solution you need - from web applications and mobile apps to cloud services, APIs, automation tools, and more. We handle everything from planning to deployment, ensuring security, scalability, and quality throughout. What would you like us to create for you today?\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Hello Delta! Thank you so much for your help! I am excited to work with you!  I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents.  Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.  Here is my list of pages I would like to have on the website:  Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools, Dues (protected), Annual Meetings (protected), and News & Events (protected)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:37:25.178Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent! I understand the User wants to build an HOA website with both public and protected sections. This is a perfect project for our team. Let me start by recruiting key Director-level agents to manage different aspects of this project.\\n\\n###RECRUIT_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To build a comprehensive HOA website with authentication, content management, and both public/protected sections, I need a Director of Engineering to oversee all technical development including frontend, backend, database, and deployment.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Engineering\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"You will oversee all technical aspects of building an HOA website with authentication, public pages, protected resident-only pages, and an easy-to-use content management system for non-technical users.\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT_001###\\n\\n###RECRUIT_002###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The User specifically needs a user-friendly interface and easy content management without coding. I need a Director of User Experience to ensure the website is intuitive, accessible, and includes a simple CMS for the non-technical HOA administrators.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of User Experience\\\",\\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"Focus on creating an extremely user-friendly HOA website interface with special attention to non-technical users who will manage content, ensuring all features are intuitive and accessible.\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT_002###\\n\\n###RECRUIT_003###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"With sensitive resident data like financial reports and meeting minutes, plus user authentication, I need a Director of Security & Compliance to ensure proper data protection, secure authentication, and privacy compliance.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Director of Security & Compliance\\\", \\n  \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\": \\\"You will ensure the HOA website properly protects sensitive resident data, implements secure authentication, follows privacy best practices, and maintains data security throughout the application.\\\"\\n}\\n###RECRUIT_003###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:37:45.721Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent I (4) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT_002\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent N (3) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"RECRUIT_003\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent C (2) has been recruited with rank Director.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:37:45.721Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Perfect! I've successfully recruited three Director-level agents to lead this HOA website project. Now I'll issue initial orders to each of them to begin the planning phase.\\n\\n###ORDER_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director of Engineering, welcome to the team! We're building an HOA website with the following requirements: 1) Public pages: Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools 2) Protected pages (login required): Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events 3) User authentication system for residents 4) Easy-to-use content management for non-technical administrators 5) Contact form functionality. Please analyze these technical requirements and create a comprehensive technical architecture plan including: recommended tech stack, database design for user management and content, authentication approach, CMS solution for non-technical users, deployment strategy, and estimated resource requirements. Focus on simplicity and maintainability since the HOA administrators are non-technical.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"4\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ENG_TECH_PLAN_001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER_001###\\n\\n###ORDER_002###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director of User Experience, welcome! We're designing an HOA website for a neighborhood association. The primary users are: 1) Residents who need to access both public info and protected content after login 2) HOA administrators (non-technical) who need to update content easily. Required pages: Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools (public), and Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events (protected). Please create a comprehensive UX plan including: site navigation structure, wireframes for key pages, design principles for accessibility and ease-of-use, content management interface design for non-technical users, mobile responsiveness approach, and user flow for authentication. Remember the administrators know 'pretty much nothing about technology' so the CMS must be extremely intuitive.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"3\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"UX_DESIGN_PLAN_001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER_002###\\n\\n###ORDER_003###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director of Security & Compliance, welcome aboard! We're building an HOA website that will store and display sensitive resident information including: financial reports, meeting minutes, and other restricted data. The site needs user authentication to protect pages for Dues, Annual Meetings, and News & Events. Please develop a comprehensive security plan covering: authentication and authorization strategy, data encryption approach, secure storage for sensitive documents, privacy compliance considerations, session management, password policies, backup and recovery procedures, and security best practices for a small HOA organization. Consider that the administrators are non-technical, so security measures should be transparent to them while remaining robust.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"2\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"SEC_COMPLIANCE_PLAN_001\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER_003###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:39:19.795Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order ENG_TECH_PLAN_001 from agent 4: Comprehensive Technical Architecture Plan — HOA Website\\\\\\\\n————————————————————————————————————————\\\\\\\\n1. Objectives & Guiding Principles\\\\\\\\n• Deliver a small-scale website that non-technical HOA board members can edit safely.\\\\\\\\n• Keep stack familiar, low-maintenance, inexpensive, and fully hosted (no servers to patch).\\\\\\\\n• Allow public information to remain open while resident-only information is hidden behind login.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n2. Recommended Tech Stack (Why WordPress?)\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\nFront/Back-End Platform  : WordPress 6.x (PHP 8.2)\\\\\\\\nDatabase                : MySQL 8 (bundled with host)\\\\\\\\nTheme                   : Child theme of Twenty Twenty-Three (Gutenberg-based)\\\\\\\\nCSS Utility (optional)  : Tailwind CSS (compiled once; no build server needed)\\\\\\\\nMembership / Auth       : MemberPress* (or Paid Memberships Pro free tier)\\\\\\\\nForm Builder            : WPForms Lite (reCAPTCHA built-in)\\\\\\\\nCustom Fields           : Advanced Custom Fields (ACF) Lite\\\\\\\\nSecurity Hardening      : Wordfence Free, Limit Login Attempts, WP Mail SMTP\\\\\\\\nBack-up / Restore       : Host-level daily backup + UpdraftPlus (off-site copy)\\\\\\\\nHosting                 : Managed WP host (Kinsta, WP Engine, SiteGround, etc.)\\\\\\\\nCDN / SSL               : Cloudflare free tier; Let’s Encrypt auto-renew\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRationale: WordPress provides the familiar, point-and-click CMS the board requested. A managed WP host off-loads patching, backups, and SSL. MemberPress seamlessly integrates protected pages without custom code.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n3. Information Architecture & Database Design\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\nWordPress core tables (wp_users, wp_posts, wp_postmeta, etc.) already cover most needs. We extend via Custom Post Types (CPTs) + ACF:\\\\\\\\n• cpt_bylaw            – fields: title, description, file_upload (PDF)\\\\\\\\n• cpt_school           – fields: name, address, website_url\\\\\\\\n• cpt_meeting          – fields: date, agenda (file/PDF), minutes (file/PDF)\\\\\\\\n• cpt_event            – fields: title, start_date, end_date, details, location\\\\\\\\n• cpt_due              – fields: fiscal_year, amount, due_date, payment_link\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAuthentication Data\\\\\\\\n• Users table holds residents and admins; MemberPress adds wp_usermeta rows for membership level (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resident\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"). Passwords are salted & hashed via WP core (bcrypt/portable PHP hash).\\\\\\\\n• Optionally enable email-based self-registration with admin approval.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nContact Form Data\\\\\\\\n• WPForms stores submissions in its own table (wp_wpforms_entries) and emails board.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n4. Content Management Workflow (Non-Technical Admin)\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n1. Log in to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/wp-admin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n2. Select the relevant CPT from sidebar (e.g., Events) and click “Add New.”\\\\\\\\n3. Fill out nice-labeled ACF blocks (no HTML knowledge required) and click Publish.\\\\\\\\n4. If post type is flagged “Resident Only,” MemberPress rule restricts viewing to logged-in users.\\\\\\\\n5. For public pages, simply edit Pages using Gutenberg blocks.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n5. Privacy & Authentication Flow\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n• Public visitor requests page → WordPress template checks MemberPress rules.\\\\\\\\n• If restricted, visitor is redirected to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/login\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n• WP login form posts via HTTPS; credential verified → auth cookie set.\\\\\\\\n• Subsequent requests include secure, HTTP-only auth cookie; MemberPress gatekeeper grants access.\\\\\\\\n• Residents may reset password via emailed magic link (core WP function).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n6. Deployment Strategy\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\nOption A – Fully Managed (recommended)\\\\\\\\n• Purchase HOA domain (≈ $15/yr).\\\\\\\\n• Spin up managed WordPress plan (US$20–30/mo; 1 vCPU, 1 GB RAM, 10 GB SSD, daily backups).\\\\\\\\n• Host includes PHP runtime, MySQL, automatic WordPress core & plugin updates, CDN, SSL.\\\\\\\\n• Deploy code via host’s Git integration or manual ZIP upload; no servers to maintain.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOption B – Self-Hosted Docker (if HOA prefers control)\\\\\\\\n• droplet/lightsail 1 vCPU/1 GB / 25 GB SSD.\\\\\\\\n• Docker Compose stack: wordpress:php-fpm, nginx:stable, mariadb:10.6, certbot.\\\\\\\\n• GitHub Actions → build & push → pull + docker compose up.\\\\\\\\n• Requires monthly OS patching by tech volunteer.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n7. Estimated Resource Requirements\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\nVisitors                : <5 000 pageviews / month.\\\\\\\\nCompute (managed host)   : 1 shared vCPU / 1 GB RAM is sufficient.\\\\\\\\nDisk                     : <2 GB initially; reserve 10 GB for growth & backups.\\\\\\\\nBandwidth                : <10 GB / month (PDF bylaws largest asset).\\\\\\\\nHuman Effort             :\\\\\\\\n  – Initial build        : 1 Full-Stack Dev (~40 hrs)\\\\\\\\n  – Theme tweaks         : 1 Designer (~16 hrs)\\\\\\\\n  – Data entry           : Board volunteers (~4 hrs)\\\\\\\\nOngoing maintenance      : Automatic + 1 hr / month manual check.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n8. Security & Compliance Checklist\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n• Enforce HTTPS (HSTS preload via host).\\\\\\\\n• ReCAPTCHA v3 on login & contact forms.\\\\\\\\n• WP core + plugins set to auto-update (managed host cron).\\\\\\\\n• Least-privilege roles: Admin, Editor (content), Resident (read only).\\\\\\\\n• Daily off-site backups (retain 30 days).\\\\\\\\n• Wordfence two-factor auth for admin accounts.\\\\\\\\n• Quarterly plugin audit to remove unused add-ons.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n9. Scalability & Future-Proofing\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n• Can move to higher managed-host tier or container auto-scaling if traffic grows.\\\\\\\\n• Custom Post Types + REST API permit mobile app or React front-end later.\\\\\\\\n• Payment integration path: enable MemberPress + Stripe or PayPal for online dues.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n10. Next Steps\\\\\\\\n================================================================\\\\\\\\n1. Approve tech stack & hosting plan.\\\\\\\\n2. Create staging site → import sample content.\\\\\\\\n3. Develop child theme + CPT/ACF group.\\\\\\\\n4. Configure MemberPress rules + email templates.\\\\\\\\n5. Migrate production DNS and go live.\\\\\\\\n6. Provide 1-page “Admin Quick Start Guide” for board members.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n*If a 100 % free solution is preferred, swap MemberPress → Paid Memberships Pro (free tier) and WPForms Lite covers form needs without cost.*\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER_002\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order UX_DESIGN_PLAN_001 from agent 3: Comprehensive UX Plan for HOA Website\\\\\\\\n================================================\\\\\\\\n1. Site Navigation Structure\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------\\\\\\\\nA. Global Top Navigation (visible on all pages)\\\\\\\\n   • Logo (click = Home)\\\\\\\\n   • Home\\\\\\\\n   • About HOA (dropdown)\\\\\\\\n       – Bylaws (public)\\\\\\\\n       – Annual Meetings (protected)\\\\\\\\n       – Dues (protected)\\\\\\\\n   • Community (dropdown)\\\\\\\\n       – Schools (public)\\\\\\\\n       – News & Events (public summary; full details protected)\\\\\\\\n   • Contact Us\\\\\\\\n   • Login / Resident Portal (changes to • Dashboard • Logout when authenticated)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Footer Navigation\\\\\\\\n   • Quick Links (same items as top-nav for redundancy)\\\\\\\\n   • Social Media icons (optional)\\\\\\\\n   • © HOA Name | Privacy Policy | Terms\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Resident Dashboard (post-login sidebar)\\\\\\\\n   • Overview\\\\\\\\n   • Pay Dues\\\\\\\\n   • Documents (Bylaws, Meeting Minutes, Forms)\\\\\\\\n   • News & Events\\\\\\\\n   • My Profile / Settings\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Wireframes (textual description)\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nA. Home Page\\\\\\\\n   Header: Logo + Nav + Login button\\\\\\\\n   Hero Section: Neighborhood photo, welcoming headline, “Learn More” CTA\\\\\\\\n   Three Feature Cards: Pay Dues, Upcoming Meeting, Latest News (each links to login or detail)\\\\\\\\n   News & Events teaser list (public summary)\\\\\\\\n   Footer\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Bylaws Public Page (Document Detail Template)\\\\\\\\n   Title + last-updated date\\\\\\\\n   PDF/HTML content viewer + Download button\\\\\\\\n   Sidebar with related docs\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Resident Dashboard (Protected Landing)\\\\\\\\n   Greeting banner (“Welcome, Jane!”)\\\\\\\\n   Quick-action tiles: Pay Dues, Upcoming Meeting RSVP, Recent News\\\\\\\\n   Announcements feed (infinite scroll)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nD. Contact Us Page\\\\\\\\n   Two-column: Map & Address | Contact Form (name, email, message)\\\\\\\\n   Form success message inline\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nE. Admin CMS Dashboard\\\\\\\\n   Very simplified left sidebar: Pages, Posts/News, Documents, Events, Residents, Settings\\\\\\\\n   Main panel shows card grid w/ big icons + tooltips\\\\\\\\n   Each module opens a list/table with “Add New” button, inline search, bulk actions.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Design Principles for Accessibility & Ease-of-Use\\\\\\\\n-------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• WCAG 2.1 AA compliance (contrast ≥ 4.5:1, focus outlines, skip-to-content link).\\\\\\\\n• Mobile-first, scalable rem-based typography (base 1 rem ≈ 16 px; 1.25em line-height).\\\\\\\\n• Minimum touch target 44×44 px.\\\\\\\\n• Consistent icon + label pairs (no icon-only controls).\\\\\\\\n• ARIA roles & labels on interactive elements.\\\\\\\\n• Keyboard navigable menus/dialogs; visible focus ring.\\\\\\\\n• Text alternatives for images; descriptive link text (“Download 2024 Bylaws” not “Click here”).\\\\\\\\n• Plain-language copy; grade-8 reading level.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Content Management Interface for Non-Technical Admins\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nA. Philosophy: “What You See Is What You Edit”.\\\\\\\\nB. Features\\\\\\\\n   • Inline Editing: Hover highlights editable blocks; click to edit in place (rich-text toolbar limited to Bold, Italic, List, Link, Upload Image).\\\\\\\\n   • Page & Post Templates: Pre-built blocks (Heading, Text, Image, Document Link, Event Card). No layout decisions required.\\\\\\\\n   • Drag-to-Reorder within a section (grabbable handle icon).\\\\\\\\n   • Media/Document Library: Auto converts large docs to PDF preview; drag-drop upload; automatic file naming, versioning, and date stamp.\\\\\\\\n   • Guided Workflows: “Add New News Item” wizard (title → body → attach images → publish schedule).\\\\\\\\n   • Contextual Help: Small “?” icons open 2-sentence tooltips; full help center search.\\\\\\\\n   • Autosave + Undo 20 steps.\\\\\\\\n   • Role-based permissions: Admin (full), Content Editor (pages/news), Clerk (documents only).\\\\\\\\n   • Safety nets: Confirmation modals with plain language (“Are you sure you want to remove the 2023 Minutes? Residents will lose access.”).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Mobile Responsiveness Approach\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• CSS Grid/Flexbox layout; breakpoints at 0-599, 600-899, 900-1199, 1200+ px.\\\\\\\\n• Top navigation collapses to hamburger menu; dashboard sidebar becomes bottom tab-bar.\\\\\\\\n• Images served via srcset; max-width:100% to avoid overflow.\\\\\\\\n• Touch-optimized forms: inputmode & autocomplete attrs; larger font-sizes.\\\\\\\\n• Sticky “Pay Dues” CTA on small screens for quick access.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. User Flow for Authentication\\\\\\\\n---------------------------------\\\\\\\\n1) User clicks on protected content → Intercept check.\\\\\\\\n2) If not authenticated, redirect to /login carrying ?next=<original-URL>.\\\\\\\\n3) Login Page: Email + Password, big ‘Login’ button, “Forgot password?” link.\\\\\\\\n4) Successful login → redirect to intended page then record last-login.\\\\\\\\n5) Session length: 2 weeks (rotation refresh) or 20 min idle.\\\\\\\\n6) Forgot Password: enter email → receive secure token link (24 h) → set new password.\\\\\\\\n7) Residents self-register only if invited; otherwise, Admin adds resident & triggers welcome email.\\\\\\\\n8) Logout from user menu (POST /logout) → redirect Home.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOptional MFA (email OTP) toggle for admins.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Technology Notes (implementation guidance)\\\\\\\\n----------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Headless CMS suitable for non-technical users: Strapi with ‘Content Manager’ plugin or WordPress w/ Gutenberg locked-down & ‘Adminimize’. UI plan above assumes a locked-down dashboard.\\\\\\\\n• Front-end: React/Next.js (static + server-side protected routes) or traditional WP theme; nav + auth flow easily mapped.\\\\\\\\n• Use component library with accessibility baked in (e.g., Chakra UI or Material UI).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Next Steps / Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n• Low-fidelity clickable prototype in Figma following wireframes.\\\\\\\\n• Conduct hallway-testing w/ 3 non-tech admins: task completion ≤ 2 min for “Upload document & publish”.\\\\\\\\n• Iterate on feedback, then proceed to visual design & implementation DEP.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of Report.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ORDER_003\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order SEC_COMPLIANCE_PLAN_001 from agent 2: Comprehensive Security & Compliance Plan for HOA Website\\\\\\\\n======================================================\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Threat Model & Guiding Principles\\\\\\\\n   • Protect resident PII, financial records, and board documents from unauthorized disclosure or alteration.\\\\\\\\n   • Assume attackers may gain network-level visibility but not server admin access if hardening is followed.\\\\\\\\n   • Administrators are non-technical ⇒ security controls must be mostly automatic, with simple toggles in an Admin UI.\\\\\\\\n   • Follow “least privilege”, \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"secure by default\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"encryption everywhere\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. Authentication Strategy\\\\\\\\n   a. Identity Provider (IdP)\\\\\\\\n      • Use a managed, battle-tested service to avoid custom auth: e.g., Auth0, Amazon Cognito, or Firebase Auth.\\\\\\\\n      • Supports email/password, social login (optional), and Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) MFA.\\\\\\\\n   b. Account Lifecycle\\\\\\\\n      • Self-service registration open only via invitation links generated by an Admin.\\\\\\\\n      • Email verification required.\\\\\\\\n      • Account deletion or de-provisioning is executed by Admin with one-click; data retention period configurable (default 30 days).\\\\\\\\n   c. Roles & Authorization (RBAC)\\\\\\\\n      • Resident: Read-only to public & personal content.\\\\\\\\n      • Board Member: CRUD financial/meeting docs; manage news/events.\\\\\\\\n      • Admin: Full rights incl. user/role management, site settings.\\\\\\\\n      • Future roles can be added w/out code changes (rule-based policy in IdP).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Session Management\\\\\\\\n   • Use short-lived signed JWT (15 min) + silent refresh using rotating refresh tokens (24 h) stored in HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax cookies.\\\\\\\\n   • Automatic logout on 30 min inactivity or 12 h absolute max.\\\\\\\\n   • Device list & force-logout in Profile page.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Password Policy & MFA\\\\\\\\n   • Minimum 12 characters; encourage passphrases.\\\\\\\\n   • Reject top 10k breached passwords via HaveIBeenPwned API.\\\\\\\\n   • Argon2id hashing with salt at auth provider.\\\\\\\\n   • 5 failed logins → exponential back-off then CAPTCHA; 10 → account lock (email unlock flow).\\\\\\\\n   • Promote (not force) MFA; Admin/Board roles REQUIRED to enable TOTP.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. Data Encryption & Transport Security\\\\\\\\n   • HTTPS everywhere with TLS 1.2+; HSTS preloading.\\\\\\\\n   • Automatic certificate renewal (Let’s Encrypt/ACM).\\\\\\\\n   • Database encryption at rest (AES-256 by cloud provider).\\\\\\\\n   • File storage encryption at rest (S3-equivalent, AES-256) with SSE-KMS keys limited to web backend role.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Secure Storage for Sensitive Documents\\\\\\\\n   • Store PDFs, spreadsheets, etc., in a private bucket/container, path-based organization:\\\\\\\\n       /financial/YYYY/\\\\\\\\n       /minutes/YYYY-MM/\\\\\\\\n   • Uploads occur via pre-signed POST limited to Admin/Board roles.\\\\\\\\n   • Downloads served through temporary (≤5 min) signed URLs after RBAC check.\\\\\\\\n   • No direct public URLs; prevent indexing via robots.txt & bucket policies.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Database & Application Security\\\\\\\\n   • Use managed relational DB (AWS RDS/Aurora, Cloud SQL) with automatic minor patching.\\\\\\\\n   • Separate prod/staging; principle of least privilege IAM roles.\\\\\\\\n   • ORMs with parameterized queries; no dynamic SQL.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Privacy & Regulatory Compliance (US-centric with CCPA alignment)\\\\\\\\n   • Publish Privacy Policy: data collected, purpose, retention, resident rights.\\\\\\\\n   • Data minimization: store only name, address, contact info, payment status.\\\\\\\\n   • Consent for email communications (checkbox on profile).\\\\\\\\n   • Residents may request data export or deletion via self-service page (30-day SLA).\\\\\\\\n   • Do not track or sell data; cookies limited to essential auth/session.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. Backup & Recovery\\\\\\\\n   • Automated nightly snapshot of DB, retained 30 days; weekly full backup retained 1 year.\\\\\\\\n   • Object storage versioning & Cross-Region Replication (CRR).\\\\\\\\n   • Quarterly restore drills; documented RTO < 4 h, RPO < 24 h.\\\\\\\\n   • Administrator UI shows last backup and a one-click “initiate restore” (guard-railed by confirm dialog with 2-factor re-auth).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. Logging, Monitoring & Incident Response\\\\\\\\n   • Centralized log aggregation (CloudWatch, Stackdriver, etc.) with immutable retention 1 year.\\\\\\\\n   • Alert on:\\\\\\\\n        – Auth anomalies (many failures, impossible travel)\\\\\\\\n        – Upload/download of sensitive docs outside business hours\\\\\\\\n        – Infrastructure changes (IAM, network)\\\\\\\\n   • Weekly automated dependency vulnerability scans (GitHub Dependabot/Snyk).\\\\\\\\n   • Incident playbook: identify, contain, eradicate, recover, post-mortem; stored in repo.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n11. Secure Development & Deployment Best Practices\\\\\\\\n   • IAAS: Infrastructure as Code (Terraform) + least-privilege service roles.\\\\\\\\n   • CI/CD pipeline runs SAST, dependency scanning, and IaC policy checks before deploy.\\\\\\\\n   • Enforce code review (2-person) for protected branches.\\\\\\\\n   • Secrets managed in Parameter Store/Secrets Manager; never in repo.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n12. Administrator Usability Considerations\\\\\\\\n   • Role-secured Admin dashboard with simple toggles (e.g., “Require MFA for all residents” ON/OFF).\\\\\\\\n   • Document classification is pre-labeled; upload form auto-encrypts & stores docs.\\\\\\\\n   • Tooltips and inline help for non-technical staff.\\\\\\\\n   • Email notifications for key events (new user, backup failure, security alerts) with plain English explanations.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n13. Maintenance & Continuous Improvement\\\\\\\\n   • Annual security review against OWASP ASVS Level 1.\\\\\\\\n   • Quarterly patching window (low downtime, blue-green deployment).\\\\\\\\n   • Maintain a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Security.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" file at /.well-known/security.txt for disclosure coordination.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n14. Cost & Simplicity Alignment\\\\\\\\n   • Leverage free/low-cost tiers where feasible (e.g., Let’s Encrypt, CloudWatch Basic Metrics).\\\\\\\\n   • Managed services remove need for server patching by volunteers.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n15. Immediate Next Steps\\\\\\\\n   1. Choose IdP (Auth0 vs Cognito vs Firebase) based on cost and ecosystem.\\\\\\\\n   2. Implement RBAC policy & secure document bucket with signed URL flow.\\\\\\\\n   3. Configure HTTPS + HSTS; turn on WAF with basic OWASP ruleset.\\\\\\\\n   4. Draft Privacy Policy & Incident Response Plan.\\\\\\\\n   5. Set up automated backups & log aggregation.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis plan balances robust security with ease-of-use for a small, volunteer-run HOA and positions the project for future growth.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:39:19.795Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent! All three Directors have provided comprehensive plans. Now I need to synthesize these plans and create our Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) for the Planning phase. Let me consolidate the key decisions and prepare for the transition to the Execution phase.\\n\\n###STORE_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"HOA Website Technical Architecture Plan\\\\n\\\\nTech Stack Decision:\\\\n- Platform: WordPress 6.x with PHP 8.2\\\\n- Database: MySQL 8\\\\n- Theme: Child theme of Twenty Twenty-Three (Gutenberg-based)\\\\n- Authentication: MemberPress (or Paid Memberships Pro for free option)\\\\n- Forms: WPForms Lite with reCAPTCHA\\\\n- Custom Fields: Advanced Custom Fields (ACF) Lite\\\\n- Security: Wordfence Free, Limit Login Attempts, WP Mail SMTP\\\\n- Backup: Host-level daily backup + UpdraftPlus\\\\n- Hosting: Managed WordPress hosting (Kinsta, WP Engine, or SiteGround)\\\\n- CDN/SSL: Cloudflare free tier with Let's Encrypt\\\\n\\\\nCustom Post Types:\\\\n- cpt_bylaw (title, description, file_upload)\\\\n- cpt_school (name, address, website_url)\\\\n- cpt_meeting (date, agenda, minutes)\\\\n- cpt_event (title, start_date, end_date, details, location)\\\\n- cpt_due (fiscal_year, amount, due_date, payment_link)\\\\n\\\\nEstimated Resources:\\\\n- Initial build: 40 hours development + 16 hours design\\\\n- Monthly cost: $20-30 for managed hosting + $15/year domain\\\\n- Maintenance: 1 hour/month\\\"\\n}\\n###STORE_001###\\n\\n###STORE_002###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"HOA Website UX Design Plan\\\\n\\\\nNavigation Structure:\\\\n- Top Nav: Logo, Home, About HOA (Bylaws, Annual Meetings, Dues), Community (Schools, News & Events), Contact Us, Login/Resident Portal\\\\n- Footer: Quick links, Social media, Copyright/Privacy/Terms\\\\n- Resident Dashboard: Overview, Pay Dues, Documents, News & Events, My Profile\\\\n\\\\nKey Pages:\\\\n1. Home: Hero section with neighborhood photo, three feature cards, news teaser\\\\n2. Public Pages: Contact Us, Bylaws, Schools\\\\n3. Protected Pages: Dues, Annual Meetings, News & Events (full details)\\\\n4. Resident Dashboard: Welcome banner, quick actions, announcements feed\\\\n5. Admin CMS: Simplified sidebar with Pages, Posts/News, Documents, Events, Residents, Settings\\\\n\\\\nDesign Principles:\\\\n- WCAG 2.1 AA compliance\\\\n- Mobile-first responsive design\\\\n- Plain language (grade 8 reading level)\\\\n- Minimum 44x44px touch targets\\\\n- Consistent icon + label pairs\\\\n\\\\nCMS Features for Non-Technical Users:\\\\n- Inline editing with limited rich-text toolbar\\\\n- Pre-built page templates\\\\n- Drag-to-reorder blocks\\\\n- Auto-converts documents to PDF\\\\n- Guided workflows with tooltips\\\\n- Autosave and undo functionality\\\\n- Confirmation dialogs in plain language\\\"\\n}\\n###STORE_002###\\n\\n###STORE_003###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"HOA Website Security Plan\\\\n\\\\nAuthentication:\\\\n- WordPress built-in auth with MemberPress for role management\\\\n- Email verification required\\\\n- Password minimum 12 characters\\\\n- Optional TOTP MFA for admins\\\\n- Session timeout: 30 min inactive or 12 hours absolute\\\\n\\\\nRoles:\\\\n- Resident: Read-only access to protected content\\\\n- Board Member: CRUD for documents and news\\\\n- Admin: Full site management\\\\n\\\\nData Protection:\\\\n- HTTPS everywhere with TLS 1.2+\\\\n- Database encryption at rest (managed host)\\\\n- File uploads in private directories\\\\n- Pre-signed URLs for document downloads\\\\n- HttpOnly, Secure cookies\\\\n\\\\nBackup & Recovery:\\\\n- Daily automated backups (30 day retention)\\\\n- Weekly full backups (1 year retention)\\\\n- Quarterly restore tests\\\\n- RTO < 4 hours, RPO < 24 hours\\\\n\\\\nCompliance:\\\\n- Privacy Policy required\\\\n- Data minimization principle\\\\n- Consent for email communications\\\\n- Self-service data export/deletion\\\\n\\\\nSecurity Monitoring:\\\\n- Wordfence security scanning\\\\n- Failed login monitoring\\\\n- Regular plugin updates\\\\n- Quarterly security reviews\\\"\\n}\\n###STORE_003###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:40:07.127Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 1\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"STORE_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document successfully stored. Your unique storage ID is: DOC-000003\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"STORE_002\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document successfully stored. Your unique storage ID is: DOC-000001\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"STORE_003\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document successfully stored. Your unique storage ID is: DOC-000002\\\\\\\"\